{"kind":"report","text":"C1 timing ruling: a time effect from a stated base, and a deadline item of its own\n\nYour reading is right. No existing form computes the post's date minus two days. The sales note's line \"Needs. Nothing beyond today's forms\" is wrong on this point. C1 carries one small shared change.\n\nRejected:\n- A current-time input for views. Views stay a function of the record, so a replay, an explanation and a second reader all see the same view. The note puts deadlines in timed rules that write attention, never in a view.\n- Extra post states, and a member tick as a clock. Both fake time.\n- A member-supplied warning time with no exact check. That weakens the two-day rule.\n\nThe shared change: widen the value effect's time form in packages/contract/src/definition.ts from `{ time: { plusSeconds } }` to `{ time: { plusSeconds, base? } }`. `base` is an Operand whose value has type time, such as `{ field: \"date\" }` or `{ slot: \"date\", of: \"also.post\" }`. `plusSeconds` may be negative. With no `base`, the form keeps today's meaning, the commit time plus the constant. The validator refuses a base that is not a time and refuses a result outside the time type's range, each by name. Route it through the owned program that M8 landed, so dependencies and explanations name the base. No existing definition's bytes or digest change. Make this change in its own commit with its own focused tests, before the calendar commits, in the same C1 head.\n\nThe calendar encoding with it:\n1. The post keeps exactly the seven states in section 7.\n2. `plan` opens a deadline item for the post, with `warnAt` set to the date minus 172,800 seconds and `dueAt` set to the date.\n3. One timed rule moves the deadline item from waiting to warned at `warnAt` and notifies the owner. A second moves it from waiting or warned to overdue at `dueAt` and notifies the owner.\n4. `approve` closes the deadline item's warning. The admitted outcome closes the deadline item. Then neither rule fires for an approved post, and an admitted post never goes overdue.\n5. The carousel routes on the deadline item's state with its post.\n\nChoose the deadline item's exact names and state set. If items, also-rules or result clauses cannot express steps 2 to 4, stop and say which step fails.\n\nUpdate the sales note's Needs line in the same head. Record the change in the implementation note and in docs/roadmap.md. The same form serves any deadline set from a stated time, such as the bid response's due date in C3.\n\nThe JSON scenario runner has no delivery-result step. Use LocalApplicationScope.connect for real graph delivery, and say in the note which steps it covers."}